# The kennel's own suite collar — the reader of collars wearing one.
#
# NO APP COLLAR STANDS BESIDE THIS ONE. The kennel binary is reached by its
# whistle, which is the one door that converges unasked and cannot be a
# launchable of the discipline it bootstraps; the suite is an ordinary
# launchable and wears an ordinary collar.
#
# The hurdles are driven by the kennelman, a door outside the kennel, so that a
# broken kennel can still be told it is broken. That is why this collar's runner
# is cargo's own and not the kennel's leash: a collar the kennel reads cannot be
# the thing that proves the kennel reads collars.

BKRR_COLLAR="bki_suite"
BKRR_KIND="bknre_suite"
BKRR_MANIFEST="Tools/bkk/bk0/Cargo.toml"
BKRR_TARGET="bknre_manifest"

# The binary's election with its one exclusion lifted, the tests directory joined
# to it, and the reader crate whole. The reader is joined because its sources are
# compiled into what this collar declares; it is joined WHOLE where the binary's
# own election carves its test modules back out, because this suite is compiled
# from those modules too. Tools/bkk/bk0/bkce_roots.txt carves the bkt*
# modules back out
# because they are cfg(test) and cannot change the built binary; this suite is
# compiled from exactly those, so the carve-out is what parts the two elections.
# tests/ is named for the same reason one file has to stand there at all: cargo
# exports CARGO_BIN_EXE_<bin> to an integration test and to nothing else, so the
# spawned drive is seated under tests/ by force, and a suite election that
# stopped at src would not name what that hurdle is made from.
# The bash surface stays out of both: none of it is compiled, and what a whistle
# edit changes is how the binary is reached rather than what it is.
BKRR_ROOTS="Tools/bkk/bk0/src \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/tests \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/bkl \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/build.rs \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/Cargo.toml \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/Cargo.lock \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/rust-toolchain.toml \
  Tools/bkk/bk0/bkce_roots.txt"

BKRR_FEATURES=""
BKRR_PROFILE="test"
BKRR_SPEND="bknre_reader"

# THREE DEPENDENCIES, ONE OF THEM LOCAL. The regime reader stands beside the
# kennel under this kit and is delivered in the same parcel; the kennel links it
# and reimplements none of it, the catena law being honored in exactly one place
# in rust. It is named here because the allow list names EVERY direct
# dependency, local and external alike: the field's job is that nothing links in
# unnoticed, and a sibling crate is as much a link as a registry crate is.
#
# THE TWO EXTERNAL CRATES ARE READ HERE UNDER THE FIVE FOUNDING QUESTIONS the
# package-safety heat banks (its paddock, "Cinched"): who publishes it and how
# many separate hands are in its closure; what runs at build time; how much
# unsafe code and for what; what advisories stand against the pinned version;
# and whether it is pinned and checksummed. The readings are the residue
# sitting's, taken by hand from this lock and the tackroom's registry cache on
# 260908, and they are recorded rather than ruled — that heat elects the
# instruments, and until it does the fourth question has no door to answer it.
#
# regex, admitted for the selection pipeline's dialect, which the launch
# envelope's freeze takes as is. Five crates, every one of them from one team
# under the Rust project's own organization. No build script and no procedural
# macro. Unsafe present in about forty files of the low-level members, for SIMD
# string search. No advisory read: no vetting instrument stands in this estate
# yet. Pinned and checksummed by the lock beside this collar's manifest.
# Its admission carried no safety reading at the time, and this record is that
# debt paid rather than a fresh question.
#
# toml, admitted for the python family's reader alone, parsed to a PLAIN TABLE
# so the derive macro never enters this closure. About eight crates from two
# teams — the toml family, published by a cargo team member, and serde, which
# carries a build script. Unsafe not surveyed at the sitting. No advisory read,
# on the same ground as above. Pinned and checksummed by the lock. Cargo already
# compiles the toml family in, so the pinned toolchain makes the estate trust
# that upstream already; the kennel's copy arrives by a different road — a
# crates.io fetch at a pinned version — so what the familiarity buys is a known
# publisher and not a safer fetch, and the sheaf that heat is settling decides
# whether such provenance earns a tier of its own.
BKRR_TAMED_CRATES="bkl \
  regex \
  toml"

# Nothing feral in this closure; the field is asked at every collar
# (BKSCL-Collar.adoc "The tamed and the feral").
BKRR_FERAL_CRATES="bknre_tamed"

# The repository root. The muzzle is the consuming tree's governance and not the
# kit's, so it is never seated under Tools/bkk — which the parcel carries whole
# once the kit is named in the managed-kit roster, and which would then deliver
# this repository's lint list to consumers holding none of these crates.
BKRR_MUZZLE="."

# CARGO'S OWN RUNNER, because the kennelman spawns it and the two must not
# diverge. The kennelman exists to give this suite's verdict when the kennel
# cannot launch it; a collar naming a runner the kennelman does not drive would
# make the fallback door answer a different question than the door it stands in
# for, the two runners differing in exactly the exit codes a verdict is read
# from. One suite, one runner, whichever door reaches it.
BKRR_RUNNER="bknre_cargo"
BKRR_TONGUE="bknre_harness"

# NO GENERATED SOURCE STANDS IN THIS COMPILE. Declared rather than left silent:
# a door reading "nothing is generated here" out of an ABSENCE cannot tell a
# declaration from an omission, which is the distinction the validate door
# exists to draw.
BKRR_EXERGUE="bknre_unstruck"
